{
  "title": "Distal Femoral Physeal Fractures - Pediatric",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pediatrics/4020/distal-femoral-physeal-fractures--pediatric",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Oct 11 2024",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Distal Femoral Physeal Fractures are common fractures in the pediatric population that result from direct trauma in children with open physes.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is confirmed with plain radiographs of the femur and knee.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4020/images/posterior_distal_femur.jpg",
              "caption": "Shows posteriorly displaced distal femur epiphyseal fracture"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is usually operative with closed reduction and percutaneous fixation followed by casting. These fractures are at high risk for the development of future growth arrest.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4020/images/treatment_of_distal_femur.jpg",
              "caption": "Shows SH II distal femur fracture stabilized with screw fixation"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "occur in patients with open growth plates",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "must be considered in patients with open physes to avoid misdiagnosis with collateral ligament injury",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Pathophysiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Mechanism of injury",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "often the result of direct trauma with some degree of rotation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "most commonly a valgus-type force or a hyperextension force",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Most commonly Salter-Harris II fracture**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4020/images/783e0c96-38cb-4c60-816a-dee72ec9b487_distal_femoral_physeal_fracture..jpg",
              "caption": "Saltar Harris II distal femur fracture"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "physis fails on the tension side",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "metaphysis fails on the compression side, creating a Thurston-Holland fragment",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "injury to physis occurs at the **zone of hypertrophy**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Osteology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "formed from a single ossific nucleus that is present at birth and is the first epiphysis in the body to ossify",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Muscles",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "both heads of the gastrocnemius and plantaris muscles originate just proximal to the physis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "leads to flexion of the distal fracture fragment when fracture line is distal to muscle insertion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Ligament",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "collateral ligaments attach distal to the physis at the level of the epiphysis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "stress places tension on the collaterals which transfer force to the physis",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "ACL and PCL attach to epiphysis at the intercondylar notch and may be injured",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Blood Supply",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "femoral artery travels through the adductor canal medially above the metaphysis and courses in the popliteal space",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "popliteal artery is directly posterior to distal femur",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "trifurcates at this level",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "due to poor collateral circulation, popliteal artery injury may result in loss of lower limb viability",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physeal considerations of the knee",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "general assumptions",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**leg growth continues until**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**16 yrs in boys**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**14 yrs in girls**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "growth contribution",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "leg grows **23 mm/year**, with most of that coming from the knee (in contrast to upper extremity where most of growth is away from the elbow)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "proximal femur - 3 mm / yr (1/8 in)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**distal femur - 9 mm / yr (3/8 in)**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "proximal tibia - 6 mm / yr (1/4 in)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "distal tibia - 5 mm / yr (3/16 in)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "History",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "history of significant trauma",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "pain with inability to bend knee",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "unable to bear weight",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "pain and swelling",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "often in flexed position due to hamstring muscle spasm",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "tenderness along the physis in the presence of a knee effusion",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "may see varus or valgus knee instability on exam",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "swelling in the popliteal space may be a sign of vascular injury or disruption",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "recommended views",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "AP",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "lateral",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "oblique",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**stress radiographs to look for physis opening if there was suspicion of physeal injury have fallen out of favor** due to risk of physeal damage, patient discomfort, and possible need for sedation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "MRI or ultrasound have replaced stress radiographs in this setting",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "indications",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "at presentation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "close follow up with repeat x-rays if inital radiographs not definitive and treating as a possible Salter I fracture",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "findings",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "physeal widening",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4020/images/91515f92-0f69-4fe3-8fa4-48d68763de86_physeal_widening..jpg",
                      "caption": "Physeal widening"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "normal 3-5mm",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "direction of displacement suggestive of mechanism of injury",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "anterior displacement due to hyperextension",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "posterior displacement due to hyperflexion",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "medial displacement due to valgus",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "lateral displacement due to varus",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "MRI",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "indications",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "diagnostic modality of choice to confirm physeal fracture",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Ultrasound",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "indications",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "can help confirm physeal fracture",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "CT",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "may be necessary for evaluation of intra-articular extension and to define fracture fragments to plan fixation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Angiography",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "occasionally necessary to evaluate for a vascular injury in the case of abnormal ABI (<0.9)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "may be necessary in fractures with wide displacement and posterior spiked fragments",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4020/images/posterior_distal_femur.jpg",
                  "caption": "Shows posteriorly displaced distal femur epiphyseal fracture"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treatment",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Nonoperative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**long leg casting**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**nondisplaced fractures**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "treated for 4-6 weeks",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "close clinical follow up is mandatory",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Operative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "closed reduction and percutaneous fixation followed by casting",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**majority of cases**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "displaced Salter-Harris I or II fractures",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "displaced fractures successfully reduced with closed methods typically should still be secured with fixation as fracture pattern is unstable",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "some Salter-Harris III or IV injuries if anatomic reduction is achieved",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "postoperatively **follow closely** to monitor for deformity",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**ORIF**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**Salter-Harris III and IV** with weight-bearing articular involvement",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**irreducible SHI and SHII fractures**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "irreducible type II fractures are most often due to interposed periosteum on the tension side of fracture",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Techniques",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Closed reduction and percutaneous fixation**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "reduction",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "avoid multiple attempts at reduction",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "reduction maneuver consists of 90% traction and 10% manipulation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "fixation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "if physis must be crossed (SH I and SH II with small Thurston-Holland fragments)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "use smooth K-wires",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4020/images/038ca16a-dcd0-4bd5-b29a-6ca4b905bba4_smooth_k-wires..jpg",
                          "caption": "Anterograde pinning of a distal femur physeal fracture with smooth-wires"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "remove 3-6 weeks after surgery",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "SH II fracture with large enough metaphyseal fragment should be fixed with lag screws across the metaphyseal segment",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4020/images/treatment_of_distal_femur.jpg",
                      "caption": "Shows SH II distal femur fracture stabilized with screw fixation"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Open Reduction Internal Fixation**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "approach",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "incision over the displaced physis to remove interposed periosteum is necessary.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "fixation should avoid the physis if possible",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "postoperative",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "usually add post-operative immobilization",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Complications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Limb length discrepancy or angular deformity**(most common)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "results from **physeal disturbance**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "limb length inequality of >2 cm in one third of cases",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "correlates with fracture pattern",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "36% of SH 1 fractures",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "58% in SH 2 fractures",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "49% in SH 3 fractures",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "64% in SH 4 fractures",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "minimize with",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "anatomic physeal alignment (critical)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "close follow up following nonoperative or operative treatment",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "treatment",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "no treatment necessary when predicted final leg length discrepancy <2 cm and no significant angulation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "indicated for discrepancy between 2-6 cm",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "epiphysiodesis of the contralateral distal femur +/- proximal tibia",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "physeal bridge excision",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "indication",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "physeal bar of <50% and",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "1",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "≥ 2 years or 2.5 cm of growth remaining",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "1",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
